WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.180
Microsoft Translator’s speech translation
technology incorporates two different Artificial

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:07.050
Intelligence systems.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:11.400
When audio first enters the system, it goes
through a Neural Network that transcribes

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.509
the audio into text.

00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:20.509
However, as we speak, we often unknowingly
repeat or use filler words such as “um”

00:00:20.509 --> 00:00:22.910
or “like” in English.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.159
The speech translation system knows this,
and removes these filler words to produce

00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:29.189
a translation that makes sense.

00:00:29.189 --> 00:00:35.129
The system uses a natural language processing
technology called TrueText which normalizes

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:41.590
the text and finds the most appropriate words
based on the context of the full sentence.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:47.920
Once the text is normalized, it goes through
a second AI system – Neural Machine Translation.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.140
This additional neural network translates
the text into the target language – in this

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.129
case, English to Japanese.

00:00:54.129 --> 00:01:00.149
If the application also offers an audio output
– such as the Microsoft Translator live

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:05.659
feature – a speech synthesizer, or Text
to Speech, will then enunciate the text and

00:01:05.659 --> 00:01:09.360
the user will be able to hear the audio translation
in their own language.

